(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["cardiac-conduction"]="<h1>Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-the-heart-need-a-conducting-system\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> Why does the heart need a conducting system?</h2>\n<p>Pumping works only if the chambers contract in the right order and at the right moment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>the atria before the ventricles</strong>, so the atria can top up the ventricles first;</li>\n<li><strong>all parts of each ventricle together</strong>, so pressure builds fast enough to eject blood.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That gives the heart two electrical jobs: <strong>start each beat</strong>, and <strong>deliver the signal in the right order</strong>. The specialised conducting system does both.</p>\n<p>Unlike skeletal muscle, the heart does not need a nerve impulse for every contraction. Specialised heart cells fire on their own; this is <strong>automaticity</strong>. The nerves only speed this built-in rhythm up or slow it down (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=7-how-does-the-autonomic-nervous-system-influence-pacemaker-activity\">§7</a>). As the lecture summarises, depolarisation spreads from the <span data-term=\"sa-node\">SA node</span> to the <span data-term=\"av-node\">AV node</span>, then down the bundle of His to the rest of the heart, and contraction starts after depolarisation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-the-pathway-of-cardiac-electrical-conduction\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> The pathway of cardiac electrical conduction</h2>\n<p><strong>SA node → atrial muscle → AV node → bundle of His → right and left bundle branches → <span data-term=\"his-purkinje\">Purkinje fibres</span> → ventricular muscle</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Structure</th>\n  <th>Where it is and what it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>SA node (sinoatrial node)</strong></td>\n  <td>In the wall of the right atrium, where the superior vena cava joins it. Starts each beat.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Internodal pathways</strong></td>\n  <td>Carry the signal across the right atrium to the AV node. Bachmann's bundle also carries it to the left atrium.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>AV node (atrioventricular node)</strong></td>\n  <td>Low in the right atrium, in the wall between the two atria, just behind the tricuspid valve. Holds the signal back briefly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Bundle of His</strong></td>\n  <td>Carries the signal from the <span data-term=\"av-node\">AV node</span> through the fibrous barrier into the wall between the ventricles.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Right and left bundle branches</strong></td>\n  <td>Run down each side of the wall between the ventricles.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Purkinje fibres</strong></td>\n  <td>Spread through the inner walls of both ventricles and hand the signal to the muscle.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs03-figure-01\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/2018_Conduction_System_of_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/2018_Conduction_System_of_Heart.jpg\" alt=\"Sectioned heart showing the SA node, atrial pathways including Bachmann's bundle, AV node, His bundle, bundle branches and Purkinje fibres.\" width=\"2021\" height=\"1354\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 1. Specialised cardiac conducting system.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2018_Conduction_System_of_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenStax College</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p><strong>What are the internodal pathways?</strong> The lecture names three: <strong>anterior</strong> (including Bachmann's bundle), <strong>middle</strong> (Wenckebach) and <strong>posterior</strong> (Thorel). They are routes the signal prefers, not insulated wires; the signal also spreads through ordinary atrial muscle. <strong>Bachmann's bundle</strong> matters most because it carries the signal across to the left atrium, so both atria contract almost together.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The three internodal tracts are named as in the lecture. Most anatomists now describe them as preferred routes through atrial muscle rather than separate insulated bundles.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"3-why-is-the-sa-node-the-normal-pacemaker\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> Why is the SA node the normal pacemaker?</h2>\n<p>Because it reaches threshold first.</p>\n<p>A working ventricular muscle cell sits at a steady −90 mV until a neighbour excites it. An SA node cell never sits still. After each beat, its voltage immediately starts <strong>creeping upwards by itself</strong> until it reaches threshold and fires. This slow climb is the <strong>pacemaker potential</strong>, also called spontaneous diastolic depolarisation: <em>spontaneous</em> (by itself), <em>diastolic</em> (between beats), <em>depolarisation</em> (becoming less negative).</p>\n<p><strong>The three phases of the SA node action potential:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phase 4: the slow climb.</strong> At its most negative, about −60 mV, the cell opens special channels that let positive ions, mainly Na⁺, leak in. This leak is the <strong>funny current (I_f)</strong>, and its channels are called HCN channels. It is called &quot;funny&quot; because most channels open when a cell becomes <em>less</em> negative, but these open when it becomes <em>more</em> negative. Later in the climb, Ca²⁺ channels start to open too, and the outward K⁺ leak fades. The voltage rises steadily towards threshold, about −40 mV.</li>\n<li><strong>Phase 0: the upstroke.</strong> At threshold, <strong>L-type Ca²⁺ channels</strong> open and Ca²⁺ rushes in. This upstroke is slower than a ventricular cell's, which is driven by fast Na⁺ channels. The SA node never gets negative enough to use its fast Na⁺ channels.</li>\n<li><strong>Phase 3: repolarisation.</strong> The Ca²⁺ channels close, K⁺ flows out, and the voltage falls back to about −60 mV. The climb then starts again.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There is no phase 1 or 2: an SA node cell has <strong>no plateau</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs03-figure-02\">\n<a href=\"https://www.mdpi.com/cells/cells-13-00004/article_deploy/html/images/cells-13-00004-g001.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://www.mdpi.com/cells/cells-13-00004/article_deploy/html/images/cells-13-00004-g001.png\" alt=\"Side-by-side SA nodal pacemaker and ventricular cardiomyocyte action potentials: spontaneous phase 4 depolarisation and calcium upstroke in nodal cells contrasted with sodium upstroke and plateau in ventricular cells, above an ECG.\" width=\"1500\" height=\"1125\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 2. Pacemaker versus ventricular action potentials.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3390/cells13010004\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Jonker, Barnett, Boink &amp; Christoffels, <em>Cells</em> (2024), Figure 1</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Voltages are typical approximate values (Guyton and Hall). The lecture covers the SA node's location and its role as pacemaker; the ionic detail is foundation.</aside>\n\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 1–3\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 1–3 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why does an SA node cell fire again without any nerve signal?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The vagus nerve releases acetylcholine that triggers each new beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Its voltage creeps up by itself after each beat until it reaches threshold</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Neighbouring atrial muscle cells excite it at the end of each beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Its resting voltage stays at −90 mV until stretch opens Na⁺ channels</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-conduction:32223361\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The SA node has no steady resting voltage. The funny current and later Ca²⁺ currents push the voltage up after every beat until it reaches threshold, so it fires again on its own.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why is the funny current called \"funny\"?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It flows only when the heart rate is unusually high</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It is carried by Ca²⁺ instead of Na⁺ like other currents</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Its channels open when the cell becomes more negative</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It flows outwards even though it makes the cell less negative</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-conduction:c3c959a5\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Most channels open as a cell becomes less negative. HCN channels open as it becomes more negative, just after a beat, and let positive ions in, which starts the next climb.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why is the SA node's upstroke slower than a ventricular cell's?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It must wait for the AV node to release the signal first</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Its gap junctions slow the entry of Na⁺ from its neighbours</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It starts from −90 mV, so it has further to climb</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It is driven by Ca²⁺ through L-type channels, not fast Na⁺</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-conduction:53de5de6\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> The SA node never gets negative enough to use fast Na⁺ channels, so its upstroke relies on slower L-type Ca²⁺ channels.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"4-if-other-conducting-cells-can-fire-spontaneously-why-doesn-t-each-set-its-own-rhythm\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> If other conducting cells can fire spontaneously, why doesn't each set its own rhythm?</h2>\n<p>The lecture puts it simply: all parts of the conducting system can fire on their own, but they are controlled by the SA node, the pacemaker.</p>\n<p>The reason is speed. The SA node climbs to threshold fastest. Each beat it starts spreads through the conducting system and fires the slower cells <strong>before</strong> they reach threshold on their own. That resets them, so their own rhythm never gets a chance. <strong>The fastest pacemaker wins</strong>; the others still have <span data-term=\"automaticity\">automaticity</span>, but they are always beaten to it.</p>\n<p>Being driven repeatedly at a faster rate also temporarily suppresses the slower cells' own firing. This is <strong>overdrive suppression</strong>, and it is why there can be a short pause before a backup pacemaker takes over.</p>\n<p><strong>What if the SA node fails?</strong> The next fastest pacemaker takes over and produces an <strong>escape rhythm</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Pacemaker</th>\n  <th>Typical rate</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>SA node</td>\n  <td>60–100 beats/min (normal resting rhythm)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>AV junction</td>\n  <td>about 40–60 beats/min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ventricles (<span data-term=\"his-purkinje\">Purkinje fibres</span>)</td>\n  <td>about 20–40 beats/min</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>An escape rhythm keeps the ventricles beating, but more slowly, so it may not pump enough blood.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-does-the-av-node-delay-electrical-conduction\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> Why does the AV node delay electrical conduction?</h2>\n<p>The AV node conducts <strong>slowly</strong>. Its cells are small, they have few gap junctions, and like the SA node their upstroke is driven by Ca²⁺. The signal takes about a tenth of a second to get through.</p>\n<p><strong>Why is that delay useful?</strong> It gives the atria time to <strong>finish contracting</strong> before the ventricles start. The <span data-term=\"atrial-kick\">atrial kick</span> pushes the last portion of blood into the ventricles while the mitral and tricuspid valves are still open. Most filling, about 70% in the lecture, has already happened passively; the kick only tops it up.</p>\n<p><strong>You can see the delay on the ECG.</strong> The lecture describes the <strong>PR interval</strong>, normally 0.12–0.20 s, as the time from the start of atrial depolarisation to the start of ventricular depolarisation. Most of that time is the signal crawling through the AV node. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/ecg?section=4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference\">→ ECG, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Why can't the signal simply spread from the atria to the ventricles?</strong> A ring of fibrous tissue between them does not conduct. The AV node and bundle of His are the only normal route across. Because the AV node can only pass impulses so fast, it also protects the ventricles from being driven by a very fast atrial rhythm.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-why-is-ventricular-conduction-so-rapid\"><span class=\"sec-num\">6.</span> Why is ventricular conduction so rapid?</h2>\n<p>After the AV node, the signal enters the <strong>His–Purkinje system</strong> and speeds up dramatically:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Tissue</th>\n  <th>Approximate conduction speed</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>AV node</td>\n  <td>about 0.05 m/s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Ordinary ventricular muscle</td>\n  <td>about 0.3–0.5 m/s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Purkinje fibres</td>\n  <td>about 2–4 m/s</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Purkinje fibres are fast because they are <strong>wide</strong>, packed with <strong>gap junctions</strong>, and use <strong>fast Na⁺ channels</strong> for their upstroke. They spread the signal over the inner walls of both ventricles in a few hundredths of a second, so the whole ventricular muscle is activated within about 0.1 s, the width of the <span data-term=\"qrs\">QRS complex</span>.</p>\n<p>The order is roughly: the wall between the ventricles first, then the inner walls near the apex, then outwards through the muscle to the outer surface, and the base last. The details belong in the ECG chapter.</p>\n<p><strong>Slow in one place, fast in another.</strong> The two speeds do two different jobs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Slow AV conduction gets the timing between chambers right:</strong> the atria finish before the ventricles start.</li>\n<li><strong>Fast His–Purkinje conduction gets the coordination within the ventricles right:</strong> all the muscle contracts together, so pressure builds quickly.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"conduction-timeline\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Follow one heartbeat's signal</figcaption><p>What it shows: a simple heart outline with the SA node, AV node, bundle of His, bundle branches and Purkinje fibres labelled. Below it, an ECG strip. Controls: Play sends a glowing signal along the pathway, and the ECG strip draws in step with it: the atria light up with the P wave; the signal pauses at the AV node, labelled \"AV delay\", along the flat PR segment; it then races down the His bundle and the Purkinje fibres, and both ventricles light up together with the QRS complex. Step buttons let students stop at each stage. Schematic timing.</p></figure>\n\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"ScZVGHDisa0\" data-media-title=\"Cardiac Conduction and ECG — Alila Medical Media\" data-credit=\"© Alila Medical Media.\" data-url=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ScZVGHDisa0\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"video-card\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\"><span class=\"vc-play\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><span class=\"vc-text\"><span class=\"vc-title\">Cardiac Conduction and ECG</span><span class=\"vc-meta\">Video · Alila Medical Media · narrated</span><span class=vc-desc>Trace SA node → AV node → His–Purkinje fibres, and notice why atrial activation precedes ventricular activation. ECG details are supplementary. The chapter text above remains the complete explanation.</span></span></button><div class=\"media-stage\"></div></div>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Conduction speeds are approximate textbook values (Guyton and Hall). The lecture gives the PR interval (0.12–0.20 s).</aside>\n\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"4\" data-part=\"Sections 4–6\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 4–6 · 4 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Every part of the conducting system can fire on its own. Why doesn't the AV junction set its own rhythm?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Its own firing is blocked by the fibrous skeleton</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Each SA impulse fires it before it reaches threshold</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It only fires when the vagus nerve stimulates it</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It fires faster, but its beats are too weak to count</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-conduction:d2000a71\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The SA node's impulse arrives and fires the AV junction before it can reach threshold by itself, resetting it every beat. The fastest pacemaker wins.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> The SA node stops firing, and the ventricles keep beating at about 45 beats/min. What is most likely setting this rhythm?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The SA node, firing more slowly because of vagal tone</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The Purkinje fibres in the ventricle walls</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A backup pacemaker in the AV junction</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Bachmann's bundle in the wall of the atria</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-conduction:59ef993e\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The AV junction's own rate is about 40–60 beats/min, so it is the next pacemaker in line. A ventricular escape rhythm is usually slower, about 20–40 beats/min.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> On the ECG, which interval mostly reflects the AV-node delay?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The QT interval</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The QRS duration</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The ST segment</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The PR interval</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-conduction:dac5ecad\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> The PR interval runs from the start of atrial depolarisation to the start of ventricular depolarisation. Most of that time is the signal crossing the AV node.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Why must conduction be slow in the AV node but fast in the Purkinje fibres?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Slow AV conduction lets the atria finish first; fast Purkinje conduction makes the ventricles act together</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Slow AV conduction saves energy; fast Purkinje conduction lets each ventricle contract twice in every beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Slow AV conduction prevents tetanus; fast Purkinje conduction shortens the ventricular refractory period</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Slow AV conduction lets the SA node recover; fast Purkinje conduction raises the overall heart rate</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-conduction:f49dd80e\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> The AV delay gets the timing between atria and ventricles right. The fast Purkinje network makes all the ventricular muscle contract together.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"7-how-does-the-autonomic-nervous-system-influence-pacemaker-activity\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.</span> How does the autonomic nervous system influence pacemaker activity?</h2>\n<p>The key rule: <strong>the faster the phase 4 climb reaches threshold, the sooner the next beat.</strong> The nerves change heart rate by changing that climb.</p>\n<p><strong>At rest, the vagus is already braking the heart.</strong> An SA node with no nerve input fires at about 100 beats/min. At rest, the vagus nerve releases acetylcholine all the time (<strong>vagal tone</strong>), holding the rate down to about 60–80. The lecture's evidence: heart rate is controlled mainly by this constant vagal discharge, so cutting the vagus (vagotomy) or blocking it with <strong><span data-term=\"atropine\">atropine</span></strong> makes the heart rate rise.</p>\n<p><strong>Sympathetic stimulation speeds the climb.</strong> Noradrenaline acts on <strong>β₁ receptors</strong> and raises cAMP inside the SA node cells. More <span data-term=\"funny-current\">funny current</span> and more Ca²⁺ current flow, the phase 4 climb gets <strong>steeper</strong>, threshold comes sooner, and the heart rate rises. Conduction through the AV node also speeds up, so the PR interval shortens.</p>\n<p><strong>Vagal stimulation slows the climb.</strong> Acetylcholine acts on <strong>M₂ receptors</strong>. It lowers cAMP, so there is less funny current. It also opens extra K⁺ channels, so the cell starts each climb <strong>lower</strong> (more negative) and climbs <strong>more slowly</strong>. Threshold comes later, and the heart rate falls. AV conduction slows, so the PR interval lengthens.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"pacemaker-slope\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>How nerves set the heart rate</figcaption><p>What it shows: the SA node voltage over 3 seconds, with a dashed threshold line. Controls: four buttons: Strong vagal (starts lower, shallow climb, about 41 beats/min); Rest (vagal tone, about 75 beats/min); No nerve input (about 100 beats/min); Sympathetic (steep climb, about 150 beats/min). The trace redraws for each, the beats-per-minute readout updates, and a one-line explanation names what changed (the slope, the starting voltage, or both). Schematic.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Two words for two effects:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chronotropy</strong> (<em>chrono</em> = time): an effect on <strong>heart rate</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Dromotropy</strong> (<em>dromo</em> = running): an effect on <strong>conduction speed</strong>, especially through the AV node.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sympathetic stimulation is positively chronotropic and dromotropic; vagal stimulation is negatively chronotropic and dromotropic.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The intrinsic SA node rate of about 100 beats/min is a standard textbook value. The lecture (Circulation 2) states that heart rate is controlled mainly by tonic vagal discharge, and that vagotomy and atropine raise it.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-clinical-significance-of-conduction-disturbances\"><span class=\"sec-num\">8.</span> Clinical significance of conduction disturbances</h2>\n<p>Electrical problems come in two kinds: the pacemaker fails, or the signal is delayed or blocked on its way.</p>\n<p><strong>The SA node fails.</strong> It fires too slowly or pauses. A backup pacemaker may take over with a slower <span data-term=\"escape-rhythm\">escape rhythm</span> (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=4-if-other-conducting-cells-can-fire-spontaneously-why-doesn-t-each-set-its-own-rhythm\">§4</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>The signal is blocked at the AV node (heart block).</strong> Think of it as three levels of trouble:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First degree: every impulse gets through, but late.</strong> The PR interval is longer than 0.20 s.</li>\n<li><strong>Second degree: some impulses don't get through.</strong> Some atrial beats are not followed by a ventricular beat; those beats are &quot;dropped&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>Third degree (complete): no impulses get through.</strong> The atria keep beating at the SA node's rate, and the ventricles beat independently at a slow escape rate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In complete block, the atria sometimes contract while the tricuspid valve is shut. Blood is pushed back into the neck veins, giving the <strong>giant &quot;a&quot; waves</strong> the lecture describes in the <span data-term=\"jvp\">JVP</span>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/pulses\">→ Pulses and JVP</a>)</em></p>\n<p>Both kinds of problem reduce pumping: the heart rate may be too slow, and the atria and ventricles no longer work in step.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"4\" data-part=\"\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">4 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> According to the lecture, atropine (which blocks vagal acetylcholine) raises the resting heart rate. What does this show?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Atropine directly speeds up the funny current in the SA node</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Sympathetic nerves cannot act on the heart at rest</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. At rest, the vagus nerve continuously slows the SA node</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The SA node needs acetylcholine before it can fire</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-conduction:9109ab64\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Removing the vagal brake lets the SA node rise towards its own rate of about 100 beats/min. So at rest, constant vagal tone is holding the rate down.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> How does noradrenaline make the SA node fire faster?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It makes the cell rest at −90 mV, closer to the Na⁺ threshold</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It steepens the slow climb of phase 4, so threshold comes sooner</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It adds a plateau, which shortens the time between beats</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It slows the AV node, so the SA node has less to wait for</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-conduction:1d825069\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> β₁ stimulation raises cAMP, which increases the funny current and Ca²⁺ current. The climb gets steeper, so each beat starts sooner.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Vagal acetylcholine opens extra K⁺ channels in the SA node. Why does this slow the heart?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. K⁺ entering the cell makes phase 0 much larger and slower</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. K⁺ blocks the gap junctions between the SA node and the atria</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The plateau lengthens, so the refractory period lasts longer</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The cell starts lower and climbs more slowly, so threshold comes later</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-conduction:00eeab9e\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> K⁺ leaving the cell pulls the voltage down, so each climb starts more negative. With less funny current the climb is also shallower, so it takes longer to reach threshold.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> In complete heart block, the atria beat at 75 per minute and the ventricles at 35 per minute. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ventricles follow their own slower escape pacemaker</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The SA node fires twice for every ventricular beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The AV node lets only every other impulse through</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The vagus nerve slows the ventricles but not the atria</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-conduction:8e8c0a6f\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> In complete block no atrial impulse reaches the ventricles. A ventricular escape pacemaker takes over at its own slow rate, independent of the atria. Letting every other impulse through would be second-degree block.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"9-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">9.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Pathway:</strong> SA node → atria → AV node → bundle of His → bundle branches → Purkinje fibres → ventricular muscle.</li>\n<li><strong>Automaticity:</strong> specialised cells fire on their own; the nerves only change the rate.</li>\n<li><strong>The SA node</strong> sits where the superior vena cava joins the right atrium. It is the pacemaker because it reaches threshold first.</li>\n<li><strong>The SA node action potential:</strong> phase 4 is a slow climb, mainly the funny current (Na⁺ in, through channels that open when the cell is most negative); phase 0 is Ca²⁺ in; phase 3 is K⁺ out. There is no plateau.</li>\n<li><strong>The fastest pacemaker wins.</strong> If the SA node fails, the AV junction (about 40–60/min) or the ventricles (about 20–40/min) take over with an escape rhythm.</li>\n<li><strong>The AV node</strong> sits just behind the tricuspid valve. It conducts slowly (about 0.05 m/s), so the atria finish contracting first. On the ECG this is the PR interval (0.12–0.20 s).</li>\n<li><strong>Purkinje fibres</strong> conduct fast (2–4 m/s), so both ventricles contract together, within about 0.1 s (the QRS).</li>\n<li><strong>Vagal tone:</strong> the SA node's own rate is about 100/min. At rest the vagus holds it at 60–80, which is why atropine or vagotomy raises the heart rate.</li>\n<li><strong>Nerves change the phase 4 climb.</strong> Sympathetic (β₁, cAMP) makes it steeper and the rate faster. Vagal (M₂, more K⁺, less funny current) starts it lower and makes it shallower, so the rate is slower.</li>\n<li><strong>Heart block:</strong> first degree, late; second degree, some dropped; third degree, none get through, with the atria and ventricles beating independently.</li>\n</ol>\n";
